# Vulnerabilities accepted for the release image scan, with an expiry review note each.
# Everything else — anything in the application's own dependency tree, and every OS package —
# still blocks promotion (see the image-scan job in .github/workflows/release.yml).

# CVE-2026-14257 — brace-expansion ReDoS, fixed upstream in 5.0.8.
#
# The only copy in the image is the one bundled inside the npm CLI
# (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/brace-expansion). The application tree resolves
# 5.0.8 via the root `overrides` block, and npm itself is never on the request path: the
# entrypoint runs `node dist/main`, and npm is present only for the documented operator runbooks
# (`docker exec openwa npm run cli …`). Reaching this code requires an operator to run an npm
# command with an attacker-chosen glob inside the container.
#
# npm 12 — the newest release, already installed by the Dockerfile — still bundles 5.0.7, so
# there is currently no npm version that clears this finding. Drop this entry once npm ships a
# bundle with brace-expansion >= 5.0.8.
CVE-2026-14257

# CVE-2026-16804 / -16805 / -16806 / -16807 — Chromium, arm64 image ONLY.
#
# These four land on `chromium`, `chromium-common` and `chromium-sandbox` (12 findings). They are
# arm64-only by construction: Chrome for Testing publishes no linux-arm64 build, so amd64 uses CfT
# while arm64 installs Debian's chromium (Dockerfile, TARGETARCH branch). Only the arm64 image
# carries the Debian package, which is why the amd64 scan is clean.
#
# THERE IS NO FIXED PACKAGE TO UPGRADE TO. The image has 150.0.7871.181-1~deb12u1; Trivy names
# 151.0.7922.47-1 as fixed, and that version exists only in Debian sid. bookworm (our base) is on
# 150.0.7871.100-1~deb12u1 and even trixie is on 150.0.7871.100-1~deb13u1 — both still vulnerable.
# Rebuilding does not clear this, and moving the base image to trixie does not either.
#
# Exposure, stated honestly rather than talked down:
#  - -16804 and -16807 are sandbox escapes. The image already runs Chromium with --no-sandbox (the
#    container is the confinement boundary: cap_drop ALL, no-new-privileges, read_only rootfs), so
#    escaping a sandbox that is not enabled buys an attacker nothing new.
#  - -16805 and -16806 are use-after-free / arbitrary code execution in Blink, and those are NOT
#    neutralised by the above. Chromium renders content the sender controls (message bodies, link
#    previews, images), so a crafted message is a plausible path to code execution as the `openwa`
#    user inside the arm64 container. This is accepted deliberately, for the release to ship while
#    Debian has no fixed build, NOT because it is harmless.
#
# Drop all four the moment bookworm ships chromium >= 151.0.7922.47 (watch
# https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/source-package/chromium) and re-run the release scan
# with them removed before assuming they are gone.
CVE-2026-16804
CVE-2026-16805
CVE-2026-16806
CVE-2026-16807
